{
  "raw": {
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191273": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor Position in Astronomy",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Natural Sciences Tenure-Track Assistant Professor Position in Astronomy Apply now Description The Department of Astronomy at The University of Texas at Austin invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the Assistant Professor level, with an anticipated start date of August 2027. We welcome applications from candidates in all areas of astronomy and astrophysics. We are particularly interested in candidates working in stellar astrophysics, time-domain and transient astrophysics, the Milky Way, the Local Group, and related areas that can take advantage of UT Austin’s exceptional observational resources, including McDonald Observatory and the Giant Magellan Telescope (GMT). Candidates in other areas that complement or expand the department’s existing research strengths are also strongly encouraged to apply. The search is primarily at the Assistant Professor level, although exceptional candidates at more senior ranks may be considered. Information on departmental and McDonald Observatory resources and research programs can be found at: https://astronomy.utexas.edu . UT Austin offers exceptional access to observational, computational, and collaborative resources. The University is a major founding partner in the next-generation 25-m GMT ( http://www.gmto.org ) . Major new facilities are also being developed at the West Texas McDonald Observatory site. UT Austin faculty have access to the 10-m HET, the 2.7-m HJST, the 2.1-m and 0.8-m telescopes, and a node for the Las Cumbres Observatory (LCO) network. Faculty participate in major astronomical surveys and observing programs using facilities including SDSS, JWST, HST, Roman, TESS, and other national and international observatories. UT Austin also provides exceptional computational resources through the Texas Advanced Computing Center ( TACC: https://www.tacc.utexas.edu ), a world leader in high-performance computing, large-scale data analysis, visualization, and machine learning. UT Austin hosts a broad range of centers and interdisciplinary initiatives that provide opportunities for collaboration across astronomy, physics, planetary science, computational science, and artificial intelligence. These include the Center for Planetary Systems Habitability (CPSH), Cosmic Frontier Center (CFC), Wootton Center for Astrophysical Plasma Properties (WCAPP), Weinberg Institute for Theoretical Physics, and the NSF-Simons AI Institute for Cosmic Origins (CosmicAI). The Department of Astronomy is a highly collaborative and collegial community, with a strong culture of mentorship, scientific exchange, and support for early-career faculty. Our faculty, researchers, and students interact across traditional subfield boundaries, and new colleagues are encouraged to develop ambitious new research directions and collaborations across the University. Beyond campus, Austin offers an exceptional quality of life for individuals and families alike, combining the cultural and culinary opportunities of a major city with abundant green space and easy access to the outdoors. Ranked among the top U.S. cities for raising a family, Austin is known for its extensive parks and trail systems, neighborhood pools and swimming holes, family-friendly restaurants and outdoor gathering spaces, and year-round cultural and recreational activities. UT Austin also offers significant resources for faculty with families, including three university Child Development Center locations. Seventy-six percent of Austin residents live within a 10-minute walk of a park, in which the city supports an extensive network of public pools and splash pads. Austin offers an unusually rich quality of life for faculty and their families. Qualifications Applicants should have a Ph.D. in Astronomy, Physics, Planetary Science, or related fields. The primary selection criteria will be excellence and leadership in astrophysics research, teaching, and mentoring of undergraduate and graduate students. We particularly seek candidates who will lead transformational science with next-generation observational and computational facilities and creatively utilize the resources of the Astronomy Department and McDonald Observatory. Applicants are encouraged to highlight throughout their application their relevant experiences and skillsets in supporting academic success of all students (e.g., their specific approaches for teaching, mentoring, research and /or scholarly work). Application Instructions Applications must be submitted electronically via Interfolio and should include: Cover letter. Statement of research accomplishments and current and future research plans (5 page limit). Candidates should describe how they envision developing their research program at UT Austin, including potential use of relevant university and departmental resources, facilities, observational capabilities, computational resources, and opportunities for collaboration. Statement of teaching experience, pedagogical approaches, and future teaching plans (2 page limit). Candidates should describe pedagogical methods they have used or would like to explore, their experience teaching at different levels, and the courses they would be prepared and/or excited to teach or develop at UT Austin. Curriculum Vitae. List of publications. Three letters of recommendation requested through the application website. Complete applications received by November 10, 2026 will be assured of full consideration. For further information about the position, please contact the search committee at astro_search2025@utlists.utexas.edu or the Department Chair, Professor Karl Gebhardt ( chair@astro.as.utexas.edu ). Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Natural Sciences Department Department of Astronomy Posted Sep 8, 2026 Apply by Nov 10, 2026 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191322": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor -- Sociology",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Liberal Arts Assistant Professor -- Sociology Apply now Description The Department of Sociology at The University of Texas at Austin invites applications for one tenure-track position at the Assistant Professor level, to begin Fall 2027. Areas of specialization are Crime, Law, and Justice, Political Sociology, or Economic Sociology, broadly defined. Duties include undergraduate and graduate teaching, including teaching in the Core Curriculum (particularly the “Foundations of Social Science\"), supporting research, programs, and collaborative projects that drive innovation in research, and service to the department, college, and university. Qualifications Applicants must have completed all the requirements for the PhD by time of appointment, or they must expect to obtain the PhD within a year of joining the faculty as Instructor. Application Instructions Interested candidates should submit a cover letter that includes a description of current and planned research, teaching statement, current curriculum vitae, writing sample, and three letters of reference to: Pamela Paxton, Search Committee Chair, Department of Sociology, The University of Texas at Austin. The committee will begin reviewing applications on October 1, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Salary is competitive and commensurate with experience and qualifications. Position funding is subject to budget availability. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Liberal Arts Department Department of Sociology Posted Sep 4, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/190886": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Practice & Program Director in Applied Data Analytics",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Education Assistant Professor of Practice & Program Director in Applied Data Analytics Apply now Description The Department of Educational Psychology (EDP) in the College of Education at The University of Texas at Austin seeks applications for a full-time professional-track Program Director for a new Option III Master’s program in Educational Psychology with a specialization in Applied Data Analytics. This innovative professional master’s program is designed to serve working professionals and prepare graduates to use data to inform decision-making across education, health, policy, and related applied contexts through a flexible, online format. This is a non-tenure track position. The Program Director will provide academic and administrative leadership for the program and play a central role in its launch, growth, and long-term success. This position offers a distinctive opportunity to shape and lead a high-impact professional graduate program at a flagship R1 institution. UT's Department of Educational Psychology has robust graduate programs and is currently ranked #6 among the Best Educational Psychology Programs rated by U.S. News & World Report . The department advances knowledge about the human experience at the intersection of education and psychology through research, teaching, and practice. It provides an interdisciplinary and contemporary approach to learning and development and seeks to enhance the quality of the human experience through rigorous educational and psychological research, and effective translation of findings to policy and practice. Responsibilities Provide strategic and operational leadership for the Option III Master’s program. Oversee curriculum implementation and continuous refinement in collaboration with departmental faculty and college leadership. Recruit, advise, and support graduate students, including working professionals and non-traditional learners. Coordinate course scheduling, instructional staffing, and instructional quality for online courses. Lead program assessment, reporting, and continuous improvement efforts. Support marketing, outreach, and enrollment management in collaboration with the College and Graduate School. Teach graduate-level courses in the Master’s program, including one or more of the following: Fundamentals of Probability and Statistics; Data Analytics: Fundamentals and Ethics; Data Wrangling and Preprocessing; Data Visualization; Regression Methods; Machine Learning for Applied Research; Applications in Data Mining; Social Network Analysis in Applied Research; Longitudinal Data Analytics; and Fundamentals of Text Analytics. Qualifications REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS Ph.D. in Data Science, Data Analytics, Quantitative Methods, Quantitative Psychology, Statistics, Applied Statistics, or a closely related field earned by January 1, 2027 Demonstrated expertise in applied quantitative methods, data analytics, or related domains. Experience with or strong potential for graduate-level instruction and/or professional training in quantitative or data-focused areas. Experience with or strong potential for leadership, program coordination, or administrative experience in academic or professional settings. Strong organizational, interpersonal, and communication skills. Appointment Details This is a full-time professional-track appointment. The position includes administrative responsibilities with associated support (e.g., course releases or equivalent workload adjustments). In addition to leadership of the Option III program, the Program Director will carry an instructional load that includes formula-funded courses in the Department of Educational Psychology. The anticipated start date is January 1, 2027. Established in 1891, the College of Education at UT Austin consistently ranks among the nation’s top public colleges of education. It is home to approximately 3,500 undergraduate and graduate students and more than 300 faculty. In addition to its longstanding commitment to academic excellence, the college has an extraordinary record of advancing innovation, opportunity, health and well-being, and educational improvement through research, teaching, and public engagement. With annual research expenditures totaling ~$60 million, the college generates knowledge with direct relevance for schools, communities, health systems, and government agencies across Texas and beyond. We also contribute to the state through the preparation of educators and health professionals and through a large network of external partnerships. Over the past five years, the college has expanded its academic programs and enrollment. The college serves students from major metropolitan areas, smaller cities, rural regions, and border communities in Texas, while also welcoming students from other states and countries. Our students diverse experiences and perspectives strengthen the college’s mission to prepare educators, professionals, researchers, and leaders who can address pressing educational and community needs. The University of Texas at Austin is the state’s leading research university, with approximately $1.3 billion in annual research expenditures and a total enrollment of more than 55,000 students. As a member of the Association of American Universities, UT Austin provides a robust ecosystem for interdisciplinary research through partnerships with the College of Natural Sciences and with many other academic and research units on campus including Dell Medical School, the School of Social Work, and the Population Research Center. The campus is situated in Austin, one of the nation’s most dynamic and fastest-growing cities, and it is in a state that offers outstanding opportunities for scholarly inquiry. Texas is home to more than 10% of the nation’s PK–12 student population, and approximately 30% of Texas students speak a home language other than English, collectively representing more than 100 languages. Texas has also experienced significant growth in its healthcare, sports, and education sectors. Together, the scale and complexity of Texas, the dynamism of Austin, and the research strength of UT Austin offer an exceptional environment for scholars seeking to advance knowledge and improve lives. Application Instructions Applications must be submitted via Interfolio and should include the following documents: Letter of application with stated qualifications Curriculum vitae Contact information including title for three references Review of applications will begin on October 1, 2027, and continue until the position is filled. Pre-employment screening includes verification of academic credentials and background check. For additional information about the department, see https://education.utexas.edu/departments/educational-psychology/ For questions regarding the application process, please contact Becky Cook ( Becky.Cook@austin.utexas.edu ). Questions about the positions can be directed to the chair of the search committee: Dr. Tiffany Whittaker ( t.whittaker@austin.utexas.edu ). Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Education Department Department of Educational Psychology Posted Sep 3, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191107": {
      "title": "Rhetoric and Writing",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Liberal Arts Rhetoric and Writing - 2026-27 Professional Track Faculty Pool Apply now Description The Department of Rhetoric and Writing at The University of Texas at Austin seeks to develop a pool of candidates for possible temporary/nontenured positions to teach undergraduate courses. Positions are not available every semester and are contingent upon student enrollment and the availability of funding. Appointments usually are made on a semester-by-semester basis, though sometimes the department has a need for an annual appointment. Positions are renewable annually, contingent upon budgetary funding and performance. Qualifications A PhD in a related field and a record of teaching excellence at the university level are preferred. Application Instructions Interested applicants should submit a current curriculum vitae, the names and contact information of three professional references, and any course materials (syllabi, course evaluations, etc.). Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Liberal Arts Department Department of Rhetoric & Writing Posted Sep 2, 2026 Apply by Aug 15, 2027 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
